### Introduction

Capitalization tells Backend what something is, instead of a keyword. UPPERCASE is a constant (`ISLAND`), Capitalized is a type (`Survivor`), lowercase is a variable or function (`name`, `push_button`). A type is declared with `Identifier { body }` -- no `class` keyword needed, the capital letter already said it.
###
Survivor {
	name,
	number,

	Self ( name, number;
		self.name   = name
		self.number = number
	)

	to_s (;
		"`name` (`number`)"
	)
}

jack := Survivor('Jack Shephard', 23)
@assert jack.to_s() == 'Jack Shephard (23)', 'a type declaration should build real instances'


### Static declarations: the hatch

`Self.` declares (and accesses) a STATIC member -- shared across every instance of the type, instead of one copy per instance. `Self.presses` is one single counter, not one per Hatch.
###
Hatch {
	Self.presses := 0

	Self.push_button (;
		presses += 1
	)

	enter (;
		Self.push_button()
	)
}

Hatch().enter()
Hatch().enter()
Hatch().enter()
@assert Hatch.presses == 3, 'every Hatch() should share the same static counter'


### Scope operators

Three forms reach into a specific scope explicitly, rather than searching outward through everything in reach: `self` is the current instance only, `Self` is the current type (static members) only, `Global` is the global scope only. Each looks in exactly one place, so a name collision elsewhere can't quietly shadow the one you meant.

`Self` alone also calls the type as a constructor -- `Self() === Type_Name()`.
###
ISLAND := 'The Island'

Dharma_Station {
	Self.station_count := 0
	station_name,

	Self ( station_name;
		self.station_name = station_name
		Self.station_count += 1
	)

	describe (;
		"`self.station_name` on `Global.ISLAND` (station #`Self.station_count`)"
	)
}

swan   := Dharma_Station('The Swan')
orchid := Dharma_Station('The Orchid')
@assert swan.describe() == 'The Swan on The Island (station #2)', 'Self.station_count should be shared, not per-instance'
@assert orchid.describe() == 'The Orchid on The Island (station #2)', 'both instances should see the same shared counter'


### Privacy convention

An identifier starting with `_` is private by convention -- reachable from inside the type's own methods, but calling it from outside raises `Cannot_Call_Private_Instance_Member`.
###
Smoke_Monster {
	_true_agenda,
	name,

	Self ( name, agenda;
		self.name          = name
		self._true_agenda  = agenda
	)

	confess ( claim;
		claim == self._true_agenda
	)
}

monster := Smoke_Monster('Smoke Monster', 'control the Island')
@assert monster.confess('control the Island') == true, 'confess should compare the claim against the private member from inside the type'
@assert monster.confess('help the survivors') == false, 'a mismatched claim should be false'
# monster._true_agenda would raise Cannot_Call_Private_Instance_Member -- outsiders don't get to ask directly


### Composition: | union

Backend has no inheritance -- types compose instead, with four operators borrowed from set theory. `|` (union) merges every declaration from the right side into the left, with the LEFT side winning any name conflicts. Multiple operators chain freely:
###
Castaway {
	survived_815 := true
}

Other {
	survivor := false
}

Jack | Castaway {
	name := 'Jack'
}

Richard | Other {
	name := 'Richard'
}

Steve | Castaway | Other {
	name := 'Steve'
}

s := Steve()
@assert s.survived_815 == true, 'a union should merge in declarations from every composed type -- from Castaway'
@assert s.survivor == false, 'a union should merge in declarations from every composed type -- from Other'


### Composition: & intersection, ~ difference, ^ symmetric difference

The other three operators only make sense once two sides might disagree. `Castaway` and `Other` both declare a `faction` method (with different bodies) here, alongside their own unique members, to show how each operator treats a genuine name collision differently:

  &  keeps only what BOTH sides declare (here: just `faction` -- the shared name)
  ~  removes the right side's declarations from the left (drops `faction`, keeps `survived_815`)
  ^  keeps only what's unique to each side (drops the shared `faction`, keeps everything else)
###
Castaway {
	survived_815 := true

	faction (;
		'Survivors'
	)
}

Other {
	survivor := false

	faction (;
		'Others'
	)
}

both := (Castaway & Other)()
@assert both.faction() == 'Survivors', '& should keep only the shared name, left side wins'

just_theirs := (Castaway ~ Other)()
@assert just_theirs.survived_815 == true, "~ should drop the shared name but keep Castaway's own, un-shared member"

unique := (Castaway ^ Other)()
@assert unique.survived_815 == true, '^ should keep what is unique to Castaway'
@assert unique.survivor == false, '^ should keep what is unique to Other'


### A composition chain needs no name at all

Any of the four operators can stand alone with no `{ }` body and no name -- `(Castaway & Other)()` above already did exactly this. It builds an anonymous type on the spot by applying the chain, exactly as if you'd written `X | Castaway | Other { }` for some unnamed `X` -- usable immediately, assignable, comparable, instantiable, without ever needing a name of its own.
###
Survivors_And_Others := Castaway & Other   # a Type value, just like any named one -- not an instance yet
survivor             := Survivors_And_Others()
@assert survivor.faction() == 'Survivors', 'an anonymous composition chain should be usable immediately, just like a named type'


### Type comparison operators

Five operators compare the composed-type SET two types or instances carry -- every type a value's own type composes, via any of the four operators above, plus its own name. All five share the `=X=` shape:

  ===  exact type-set equality
  =!=  negation of ===
  =>=  is left a superset of right (left composes at least everything right does)
  =<=  mirror of =>= with operands reversed
  =/=  disjoint -- the two share no composed type at all

Only `=>=` carries genuinely new information; the rest are readability sugar built on it, the same reason most languages ship both `<=`/`>=` alongside `==`/`!=`.
###
@assert (Jack === Jack) == true, '=== should be true for identical composed-type sets'
@assert (Jack === Richard) == false, '=== should be false -- Jack composes Castaway, Richard composes Other'
@assert (Jack =!= Richard) == true, '=!= should be the negation of ==='
@assert (Steve =>= Castaway) == true, '=>= should be true -- Steve composes with at least everything Castaway does'
@assert (Castaway =>= Steve) == false, "=>= should be false -- Castaway doesn't compose Steve's extra (Other) side"
@assert (Castaway =<= Steve) == true, '=<= should be the mirror of the line above'
@assert (Jack =/= Richard) == true, '=/= should be true -- Castaway vs Other share no overlap'
@assert (Castaway =/= Other) == true, '=/= should be true -- the two traits themselves share nothing either'
